<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Harusato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Matsudaira Fumai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Harusato was the seventh lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> domain in Izumo, better known by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> name Fumai. With his ministers he restored the domain's finances through land reform, flood control and ginseng production. He became one of the great tea masters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, founding a school of tea and assembling a famous collection of tea utensils.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-harusato.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Harusato" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsudaira_Fumai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harusato was born in 1751 at the Matsudaira residence in Edo, present-day Tokyo, the second son of Matsudaira Munenobu, who then ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> domain. When Munenobu, the sixth lord, retired in 1769, the sixth year of the Meiwa era, Harusato succeeded him. By then the domain had been reduced to poverty, largely by the contributions the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> had ordered for the repair of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With the support of his chief retainer, Asahi Tamba, Harusato set out to reverse the domain's decline quickly. He raised the production of its main products and made its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddies more secure by promoting flood control. These efforts succeeded, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> was among the fastest of the domains to complete its reforms. The relationship was not without friction: Asahi criticised him for spending domain funds on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> was established, the practice of chanoyu by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> had lost its appeal and its role as a focus of political ties among them. From about the middle of the 18th century the older samurai who had scorned it as a pastime of idle men had died, and a new generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> were leading the development of urban culture. Harusato stood out among them.</p><p>In 1770, early in his reform of the domain, he wrote a treatise called Mudagoto, Useless Words, apparently in answer to Asahi's criticism. In it he wrote that making chanoyu a luxury and exhausting beauty to make it splendid was a distressing thing, and that it could instead be an aid to governing the country well. His tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> descended through Isa Kotaku, a disciple of the Rinzai Zen monk Ikei Soetsu, abbot of the Daitokuji temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, who had learned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and tea master Katagiri Sadamasa Sekishu. From Arai Itsusho, an Edo devotee in the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadaoki">Hosokawa Tadaoki</a>, he also obtained a copy of the Nanporoku, a record of the teachings of Sen no Rikyu.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matsudaira Harusato” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Matsudaira Harusato</b>, known as Fumai, restored the domain's finances in the late 18th century and became a leading tea master and collector.</blockquote><a href="/state/matsue-domain">Matsue Domain</a></li></ul></section>
